---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code} — ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    In the first session you open a position and file four answers you are not
    allowed to revise. The position stays open until the last week.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each week after that names one mechanism by which money is lost, derives it
    in front of you, and asks you to check your own position against it. Some of
    the mechanisms you can commit deliberately; the rest have to be handed to you
    by the world, and the course is explicit about which is which. You keep a
    <a href={withBase("/records/")}>position log</a> throughout, because a loss
    you cannot reconstruct is worth nothing here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twice you write it up: once for a single loss, accounted for to the cent, and
    once for the whole semester as a decomposition. What you leave with is an
    itemised ledger of your own decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>What this course does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not teach you to make money, and it does not imply that the attempt
    would work. Every mechanism on the syllabus is a way to lose, every worked
    example ends lower than it started, and the final week's argument is that a
    student who finished the semester ahead has learned nothing from having done
    so.
  </p>
  <p>
    The losses are real and they are bounded. Paper positions are the default;
    real money is an opt-in track with a hard cap, and positions that can cost
    you more than you put in are not permitted. The
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>bounded-loss policy</a> is a course rule,
    not a caution, and it is worth reading before the first session.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Someone who has never held a security. No finance is assumed, no statistics
    beyond arithmetic, and every number on the site is one you can reproduce from
    the inputs printed beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you already trade, this course has nothing to tell you about what to buy.
    It may still be the first time anyone has asked you to total the counterfactual
    column.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>Every week, and the one named failure mode it owns.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The four checkpoints: opening, crit, midpoint, close.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>One loss accounted for, then the semester decomposed.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>What the loss requirement is, and what caps it.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
